1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,100
It is the 22nd of January,

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:05,220
2003. At the Johnson Space Center,

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:08,840
an engineer named Rodney Rocha is typing an email he will not send.

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:14,420
Six days earlier, 81.7 seconds after Columbia left the pad,

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,280
a piece of insulating foam came off the fuel tank.

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,040
Two -tenths of a second later,

7
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,880
it struck the underside of the left wing.

8
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,740
Nobody is sure how big it was.

9
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,480
The cameras that should have recorded it were out of focus,

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:27,920
or pointed the wrong way,

11
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:29,660
or lost the vehicle altogether.

12
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,360
So Rocha asks for a photograph of the wing.

13
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,360
He asks on flight day four and gets no answer.

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,640
He asks again on flight day six.

15
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,080
And on the morning of the 22nd,

16
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:45,000
he is told the shuttle program is not requesting any outside imaging help.

17
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:48,620
So he writes it down, that this is the wrong answer,

18
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:50,240
that it borders on irresponsible,

19
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:55,940
and at the end, this. Remember the NASA safety posters everywhere around

20
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:57,620
stating, if it's not safe,

21
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,200
say so? Yes, it's that serious.

22
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:03,760
He printed it out, carried it down the hall,

23
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:05,060
and showed it to a colleague.

24
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:07,440
He never sent it. Asked why,

25
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:10,860
he told the accident board he did not want to jump the chain of command.

26
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:14,320
Six months later, in San Antonio,

27
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:19,060
engineers loaded a block of that foam into a nitrogen cannon built for testing bird strikes

28
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:22,460
and fired it at a shuttle wing at 530 miles an hour.

29
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:27,140
This is what it did, a hole roughly 16 inches across,

30
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:28,720
straight through the leading edge.

31
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:30,980
The photograph Rodney Rocha asked for,

32
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,100
three times, was never taken.

33
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,260
Columbia, the photographs NASA never took.

34
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:40,840
To understand why nobody took that photograph,

35
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,820
you have to go back 22 years,

36
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,120
to the first time a space shuttle ever flew.

37
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:50,720
April, 1981. Columbia is the first orbiter,

38
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:52,640
and this is the first flight of the program.

39
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:54,720
It comes back with damaged tiles.

40
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:58,580
The external tank is covered in insulating foam,

41
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:02,320
because it holds liquid hydrogen at 253 degrees below zero,

42
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:04,000
and it would otherwise ice over.

43
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:07,000
The design requirement was explicit.

44
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:09,280
The tank was not supposed to shed anything.

45
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,800
Not foam, not ice, nothing.

46
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,800
It shed foam on the first flight,

47
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:18,940
and on the second, and it kept doing it for 22 years,

48
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:21,340
on flight after flight after flight.

49
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,560
There is a particular place on the tank called the left bipod ramp,

50
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:29,340
a wedge of hand sprayed foam over the strut that holds the orbiter's nose.

51
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,360
Before Columbia's last flight,

52
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,260
foam had come off that exact ramp at least six times.

53
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:38,040
But here is the part that matters.

54
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:41,240
At the flight readiness review before Columbia launched,

55
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,540
managers were told there had been only three.

56
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:46,820
Two of the six had not been found yet.

57
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:48,960
The accident board discovered them afterwards,

58
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:50,360
going back through the record.

59
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:54,220
The people approving the flight were working from a number that was wrong by half.

60
00:02:56,100 --> 00:03:01,600
December, 1988. Atlantis comes back from a classified mission with more than 700 damaged

61
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:03,540
tiles and one tile missing altogether.

62
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,900
The commander later said he believed they were going to die on re -entry.

63
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:11,460
The damage was directly beneath an antenna mounting plate,

64
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:15,160
which was made of steel, and that is the only reason the wing survived.

65
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:20,220
October, 2002, three months before Columbia,

66
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:24,960
foam comes off the left bipod ramp and hits the solid rocket booster attach ring,

67
00:03:25,260 --> 00:03:27,360
about four inches wide and three inches deep.

68
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:30,480
That was the closest warning anyone got,

69
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:32,900
and it arrived two missions before the accident.

70
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:34,880
The program classified it,

71
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:37,420
flew twice more, and did not fix the ramp.

72
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:40,320
There is a word for what was happening,

73
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,080
and the accident board used it.

74
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:44,440
NASA had two categories for problems,

75
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:46,920
in -family and out -of -family.

76
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:52,040
In -family meant a problem you had seen before within the experience base.

77
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:54,560
Out -of -family meant something new.

78
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,060
Foam loss had been seen so many times that it had become,

79
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:00,420
by definition, in -family.

80
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:05,640
The board called that a strange term indeed for a violation of system requirements,

81
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,680
because the classification had been introduced after Challenger to help.

82
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:14,560
And by definition, the problems moved into the lesser category got less attention.

83
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:18,940
The sociologist who worked on this report has a name for it,

84
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:20,680
the normalization of deviance.

85
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,620
You see the thing that should not happen,

86
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,160
and it does not kill you,

87
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:26,620
so it stops being the thing that should not happen.

88
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:30,620
On the 16th of January, 2003,

89
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:34,680
seven people boarded that orbiter for a 16 -day science mission.

90
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:38,560
Rick Husband, Commander. William McCool,

91
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:41,340
Pilot. Michael Anderson, Payload Commander.

92
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,480
Kalpana Chala, Flight Engineer.

93
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:45,720
David Brown. Laurel Clark.

94
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,420
And Elon Ramone, the first Israeli in space.

95
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:51,160
It was not a station flight.

96
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:53,620
There was no docking, no rendezvous.

97
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:57,140
Columbia carried a laboratory module in the payload bay,

98
00:04:57,540 --> 00:05:00,040
and the crew ran experiments around the clock in two shifts.

99
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:03,400
That detail matters more than it sounds.

100
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,160
Because Columbia was not going to the space station,

101
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:08,040
it had no robotic arm fitted,

102
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:10,740
and there was nowhere to shelter if anything went wrong.

103
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,940
81.7 seconds after launch,

104
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,600
the foam came off. Nobody saw it happen.

105
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:20,060
Not in the control room, not on the pad,

106
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:24,820
not anywhere. It was found the next day when the routine film review caught it.

107
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:27,980
And when they went looking for a clear picture of it,

108
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:33,220
they did not have one. Of the ground camera sites that should have recorded the ascent,

109
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:35,060
the first lost track of the vehicle.

110
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:38,540
The second was out of focus because of an improperly maintained lens.

111
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:40,900
The third caught only the top of the wing.

112
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:44,800
The accident board added one sentence to that finding.

113
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:48,180
Camera problems also hindered the Challenger investigation.

114
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:51,320
So on the 17th of January,

115
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:53,960
the chair of the photo working group picked up the phone.

116
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:56,180
The strike was classified out of family.

117
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,880
Something new. Something nobody had seen.

118
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:01,660
A team was stood up to analysis.

119
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:03,840
It was called the debris assessment team,

120
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:06,180
and it was co -chaired by Rodney Rocha.

121
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:11,400
Over the next five days, three separate people asked for a photograph of that wing.

122
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,440
Three separate requests through three separate channels.

123
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:18,740
The board counted them and printed them in a box in the report,

124
00:06:18,980 --> 00:06:20,920
so there could be no argument about it afterwards.

125
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:24,300
Request one, flight day two.

126
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:27,140
Bob Page, chair of the photo working group,

127
00:06:27,460 --> 00:06:30,500
to Wayne Hale, shuttle program manager for launch integration,

128
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:35,080
in person. Request two, flight day six.

129
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:38,580
Bob White, a contractor manager to Lambert Austin,

130
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:39,940
head of systems integration,

131
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:44,420
by phone. Request three, flight day six.

132
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:46,500
Rodney Rocha, to Paul Schack,

133
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:48,520
manager of the shuttle engineering office,

134
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:52,200
by email. Rocha's email is worth reading,

135
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:54,400
because of one word in it.

136
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,100
Can we petition, and then in brackets,

137
00:06:57,860 --> 00:06:59,840
beg for outside agency assistance?

138
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:03,800
He was the chief engineer for the thermal protection system,

139
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:07,340
the heat shield, the thing that keeps the vehicle from burning up,

140
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:09,420
and he is using the word beg.

141
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:13,060
None of the three requests went through the normal channel,

142
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:14,880
which ran through mission management.

143
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:17,600
The engineers went up the engineering side instead,

144
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:19,500
because management was not engaging.

145
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,960
And the board found that this routing was itself part of the failure.

146
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:29,000
Routing the request through the engineering department led in part to it being viewed

147
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,480
by shuttle program managers as a non -critical engineering desire,

148
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:34,800
rather than a critical operational need.

149
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:38,100
On the morning of the 22nd of January,

150
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:42,060
the United States military began preparing to photograph Columbia in orbit,

151
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:43,860
90 minutes later it stopped.

152
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:49,280
Wayne Hale phoned a defense department representative at the Cape and asked the military

153
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:52,580
to start the process. Within an hour,

154
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:56,940
that request reached United States strategic command at Cheyenne Mountain in Colorado.

155
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:01,160
A plans officer there began identifying which assets could do it.

156
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:04,400
This is real. It was happening.

157
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:08,580
Somewhere in Colorado, people were working out how to point something at a wing.

158
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,420
At half past eight that morning,

159
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:15,640
NASA's liaison officer called strategic command back and canceled it.

160
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:20,960
The reason given was that NASA had identified its own in -house resources and no longer

161
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:22,200
needed the military's help.

162
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:26,540
The board wrote the next sentence in a way that leaves nothing to interpret.

163
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:31,140
The NASA request to the Department of Defense to prepare to image Columbia on orbit was

164
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:33,400
both made and rescinded within 90 minutes.

165
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,720
What happened inside those 90 minutes,

166
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:38,440
the board describes carefully,

167
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:42,860
and so will we. It says the following sequence likely occurred.

168
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:46,060
The chair of the mission management team,

169
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:48,860
Linda Hamm, asked who was requesting the imagery.

170
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:51,040
She called several senior managers.

171
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:55,480
Each said they had not requested it and could not identify a requirement for it.

172
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:58,140
And that word, requirement,

173
00:08:58,620 --> 00:08:59,720
is where this comes apart.

174
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,640
Because the engineers had not filed a requirement,

175
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:04,240
they had asked a question.

176
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:08,080
The board's finding is narrow and it is precise.

177
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:12,720
Hamm has publicly said she did not know the debris assessment team wanted the images.

178
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:14,760
The board does not dispute that.

179
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:19,960
What it says is this. She never asked them directly if the request was theirs,

180
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:22,460
even though they were the team analyzing the foam strike.

181
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:25,400
There is one more finding here,

182
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:27,560
and it is the one almost nobody quotes.

183
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:32,680
Nobody in the chain of command had the security clearance to know what American imaging

184
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:34,140
satellites could actually do.

185
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:36,700
Not at NASA, not at the contractors.

186
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:40,020
Nobody asked what quality of picture was even possible.

187
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:45,180
So the decision not to look was made by people who did not know what looking would have

188
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:49,200
shown them. The board's phrase is that they were making critical decisions about imagery

189
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,180
capabilities based on little or no knowledge.

190
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:56,420
On the 23rd of January, a flight director wrote an email closing the matter.

191
00:09:57,060 --> 00:10:00,120
The shuttle program was asked directly if they wanted outside resources.

192
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:03,340
They said no. After talking to Phil,

193
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:05,280
I consider it to be a dead issue.

194
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,840
The flight director who wrote that was Leroy Kane.

195
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,320
Remember the name. He comes back at the end of this.

196
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:14,940
While all of that was happening,

197
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:17,400
the engineers were doing the only thing left to them.

198
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,820
They were trying to calculate the answer instead of photographing it.

199
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:23,120
The tool was called Crater.

200
00:10:23,780 --> 00:10:27,600
It was a mathematical model for predicting how deep a piece of debris would penetrate

201
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:31,820
the tiles. It had been built and validated for small debris.

202
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:35,580
Ice, mostly. Pieces about three cubic inches.

203
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:38,480
The piece that hit Columbia was,

204
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:40,280
by the board's later estimate,

205
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:44,320
about 400 times larger than anything Crater had ever been tested against.

206
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:48,380
The engineers running it that week thought the figure was 640.

207
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:53,420
Crater came back and said the debris would go deeper than the tile itself,

208
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:55,320
which would mean burn through.

209
00:10:56,800 --> 00:10:59,520
And here's the part that gets told wrong almost everywhere.

210
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:02,060
Management did not overrule that result.

211
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:06,020
The engineers did. They knew Crater ran conservative.

212
00:11:06,020 --> 00:11:09,320
They knew it did not model the hardened lower layer of the tile.

213
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:13,820
So they talked themselves down on reasoning that was defensible and turned out to be

214
00:11:13,820 --> 00:11:17,260
wrong. What they could not do was check that reasoning.

215
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:19,220
To check it, they needed a picture.

216
00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:23,160
The board wrote one sentence about this situation that is,

217
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:26,640
I think, the single most important line in the entire report.

218
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:31,520
The debris assessment team was put in the untenable position of having to prove that

219
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:36,380
a safety of flight issue existed without the very images that would permit such a determination.

220
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:39,580
Operations must be proved safe,

221
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:41,040
rather than the other way around.

222
00:11:41,920 --> 00:11:43,540
NASA inverted this burden of proof.

223
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:49,040
Meanwhile, two engineers were e -mailing each other about what would happen if the landing

224
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:50,900
gear bay burned through on the way down.

225
00:11:52,060 --> 00:11:56,900
On the 27th of January, a landing gear specialist at Langley wrote back to a colleague

226
00:11:56,900 --> 00:12:01,260
at Houston, I would bail out before I would let a loved one land like that.

227
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:03,880
The next day he wrote again,

228
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:08,360
any more activity today on the tile damage or people just relegated to crossing their

229
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:09,660
fingers and hoping for the best?

230
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:12,240
Those e -mails went sideways,

231
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:14,960
between two engineers. They did not go up.

232
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:19,480
Program rules said the mission management team would meet every day of a flight.

233
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:21,560
During Columbia's 16 days,

234
00:12:22,060 --> 00:12:26,820
it met five times. Two safety officials were contacted about the strike.

235
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:30,000
One of them was the highest ranking safety official at NASA.

236
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,320
The board's finding is that safety personnel took no actions to obtain imagery.

237
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:38,180
And the crew. People often say the crew never knew.

238
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:42,200
That is not true. On the 23rd of January,

239
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:44,680
a flight director emailed the commander and the pilot,

240
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:46,480
sent them the video of the strike,

241
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:50,800
and told them it was not even worth mentioning and that there was absolutely no concern

242
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:53,520
for entry. Husband wrote back and thanked him.

243
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:55,880
They knew about the phone.

244
00:12:56,640 --> 00:13:00,180
What they were never told was that three people had asked for a photograph of their wing

245
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:02,280
and that the request had been called off.

246
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:05,620
Saturday, the 1st of February,

247
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:09,080
2003. Entry interface over the Pacific,

248
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:11,820
844 in the morning, Eastern time.

249
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:14,700
Columbia comes in over California,

250
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,020
then Nevada, then New Mexico,

251
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:20,020
trailing plasma. On the ground in Texas,

252
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:23,700
people are filming it because it is a nice thing to watch on a Saturday morning.

253
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:30,260
At 854 in 24 seconds, a console officer in Mission Control calls the flight director.

254
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:35,920
FYI, I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the left side of the vehicle,

255
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:37,580
hydraulic return temperatures.

256
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:42,460
I mean, you're telling me you lost them all at exactly the same time?

257
00:13:43,620 --> 00:13:46,320
All four of them are located in the aft part of the left wing,

258
00:13:46,860 --> 00:13:47,880
right in front of the elevons,

259
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:50,820
elevon actuators, and there is no commonality.

260
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:55,440
No commonality. Meaning the sensors are not wired together,

261
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:58,320
so a single wiring fault cannot explain it,

262
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:00,440
meaning something is happening to the left wing.

263
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:04,720
We just lost tire pressure on the left outboard and left inboard,

264
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:08,460
both tires. In Columbia, Houston,

265
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:12,260
we see your tire pressure messages and we did not copy your last call.

266
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:15,380
The answer comes back from the flight deck,

267
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:17,740
one word and the beginning of another.

268
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:21,000
The report prints it as Roger,

269
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,360
and then, in square brackets,

270
00:14:23,820 --> 00:14:28,180
cut off in mid -word. 859 and 32 seconds,

271
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:31,800
loss of signal, which, at that point in an entry,

272
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:35,720
is completely normal. It happens every flight when the antennas switch.

273
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:38,620
So nobody in that room reacts.

274
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:40,960
They wait the way they always wait.

275
00:14:42,340 --> 00:14:45,820
46 seconds later, the vehicle comes apart over east Texas.

276
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:47,880
Mission Control has no idea.

277
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:50,380
They are still working the tire pressure problem.

278
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:55,880
For 12 more minutes, the loop carries an ordinary technical conversation about instrumentation,

279
00:14:56,720 --> 00:14:59,820
while outside, in Nacog Doaches and Hemphill and Lufkin,

280
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:02,500
people are hearing what sounds like thunder on a clear day.

281
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,500
At about 12 minutes past 9,

282
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:09,520
a member of the Flight Control team gets a call on his mobile phone from someone who

283
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:10,640
has just seen it on television.

284
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:14,260
He walks over to the flight director's console and tells him.

285
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:18,440
Flight director Leroy Kane calls for the ground control officer.

286
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:22,020
He has to call twice. Lock the doors.

287
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:25,920
Then he tells the room, no phone calls off -site,

288
00:15:26,260 --> 00:15:29,960
no data in or out. Every console begins archiving its own data,

289
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:31,220
because from this moment,

290
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:36,520
the room is evidence. What follows is the part the accident board actually spent most

291
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:38,520
of its time on, not the phone,

292
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:42,540
the organization. First, the search.

293
00:15:43,180 --> 00:15:46,240
More than 25 ,000 people from 270 organizations,

294
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:48,460
over one and a half million hours,

295
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:50,500
across 2.3 million acres,

296
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:52,560
an area approaching the size of Connecticut.

297
00:15:54,020 --> 00:15:55,840
84 ,000 pieces were recovered.

298
00:15:56,460 --> 00:16:01,040
84 ,900 pounds, 38 % of the orbiter.

299
00:16:01,860 --> 00:16:04,860
Satellites, infrared, hyperspectral imaging,

300
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:06,620
ground -penetrating radar,

301
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:08,620
and U -2 aircraft were all used.

302
00:16:08,620 --> 00:16:11,540
The board's assessment is that they proved of little value.

303
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:14,360
It came down to people walking in a line through the woods.

304
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:20,700
On the 27th of March, a helicopter searching Angelina National Forest suffered a mechanical

305
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:22,780
failure and went down at treetop height.

306
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:24,720
It was too low to autorotate.

307
00:16:26,340 --> 00:16:28,140
Jules Meir, a contract pilot,

308
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:31,800
and Charles Krenek, a Texas Forest Service aviation specialist,

309
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:34,240
were killed. Three others were injured.

310
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:39,060
The accident board put both of their names on the dedication page of its report,

311
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:41,920
with their job titles underneath the seven astronauts.

312
00:16:42,580 --> 00:16:44,520
Nine people died because of this accident,

313
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:46,620
and the report says so on the first page.

314
00:16:48,120 --> 00:16:52,120
Then, in July, they went to San Antonio and fired the foam at the wing.

315
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:58,700
1.67 pounds of foam at 777 feet per second into a

316
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:01,560
panel taken from Atlantis that had flown 26 missions.

317
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:05,180
A hole roughly 16 inches by 17,

318
00:17:05,180 --> 00:17:07,560
cracks running 11 inches out from it.

319
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:11,120
And the board wrote down what the test was actually for,

320
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:14,020
which is the most revealing sentence in that whole chapter.

321
00:17:15,620 --> 00:17:20,100
The impact tests established that foam can breach the reinforced carbon -carbon and also

322
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:23,480
counteracted the lingering denial or discounting of the analytic evidence.

323
00:17:24,860 --> 00:17:27,820
They did not fire that gun to find out whether foam could break a wing.

324
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:30,020
The analysis already said it could.

325
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:33,240
They fired it to end an argument inside their own organization.

326
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:36,160
Because five months earlier,

327
00:17:36,640 --> 00:17:37,880
four days after the accident,

328
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:42,180
the shuttle program manager had told a press conference that it doesn't make sense to

329
00:17:42,180 --> 00:17:46,520
us that a piece of debris could be the root cause of the loss of Columbia and its crew.

330
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:50,740
That was not a lie. It was what the analysis appeared to show,

331
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,120
to people who had watched foam fall off that tank for 22 years without anyone dying.

332
00:17:56,540 --> 00:17:58,220
The board's conclusion is one sentence,

333
00:17:58,380 --> 00:18:00,180
and it is the reason this episode exists.

334
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:06,080
In the board's view, NASA's organizational culture and structure had as much to do with

335
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:07,880
this accident as the external tank foam.

336
00:18:09,240 --> 00:18:10,720
And they knew how that would be received,

337
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:12,580
so they wrote a warning about it first.

338
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:17,320
Many accident investigations make the same mistake in defining causes.

339
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:19,440
They identify the widget that broke,

340
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:22,960
then locate the person most closely connected with the technical failure.

341
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,120
The board did not want to make these errors.

342
00:18:26,540 --> 00:18:30,120
Nobody was prosecuted. Some managers were reassigned that July,

343
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:34,500
and NASA said publicly on the day that the changes were no reflection on the competence

344
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:37,140
or diligence or commitment or professionalism of anybody.

345
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:42,220
So the organization restructured while stating that no individual was at fault,

346
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:44,620
which is exactly what the board asked for,

347
00:18:45,060 --> 00:18:47,160
and it is also why no one was ever held to account.

348
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:49,240
Both of those things are true at once.

349
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:52,160
There is one more thing in this report,

350
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:54,300
and it is the strangest fact I found.

351
00:18:55,300 --> 00:19:00,540
In 1996, a sociologist named Diane Vaughn published a book about the Challenger

352
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:04,160
accident. She argued that the disaster was not caused by villains,

353
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:08,060
but by an organization slowly agreeing that a dangerous thing was normal.

354
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:10,440
She called it the normalization of deviance.

355
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:16,700
In April 2003, she was called to testify before the Columbia Board in Houston.

356
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:19,720
Then they hired her onto the staff.

357
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,580
And then she personally wrote chapter 8 of the report,

358
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:26,260
the chapter is called History as Cause,

359
00:19:27,020 --> 00:19:32,020
Columbia and Challenger. The woman who diagnosed the first accident was brought in to

360
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:33,620
write the finding that nobody had listened.

361
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:39,620
Her chapter says this, the causes of the institutional failure responsible for Challenger

362
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:41,900
have not been fixed. And second,

363
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:44,700
that if these systemic flaws are not resolved,

364
00:19:45,120 --> 00:19:46,780
the scene is set for another accident.

365
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:51,340
There is an image in that chapter I have not been able to stop thinking about.

366
00:19:51,340 --> 00:19:56,080
Engineers and managers incorporated worsening anomalies into the engineering experience

367
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:58,960
base, which functioned as an elastic waistband,

368
00:19:59,260 --> 00:20:02,200
expanding to hold larger deviations from the original design.

369
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:06,220
The word echoes, which that chapter turns on,

370
00:20:06,560 --> 00:20:11,300
came from Sally Ride. She sat on the Challenger Commission in 1986 and on the Columbia

371
00:20:11,300 --> 00:20:14,600
Board in 2003, the only person who ever did both.

372
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:19,820
And the closest parallel the report draws between the two accidents is a single sentence.

373
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:23,600
Challenger astronauts were told that the cold temperature was not a problem,

374
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:27,160
and Columbia astronauts were told that the foam strike was not a problem.

375
00:20:28,700 --> 00:20:30,220
The board made 29 recommendations.

376
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:33,200
15 had to be done before anyone flew again.

377
00:20:34,460 --> 00:20:38,000
An independent task group later found NASA met the intent of 12 of them.

378
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:43,160
Three, it could not. One of the three was hardening the orbiter's wing.

379
00:20:43,700 --> 00:20:45,560
And the stated reason it was dropped is this.

380
00:20:46,060 --> 00:20:50,160
The long -term plan was abandoned after the national policy decision to retire the space

381
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:51,780
shuttle fleet no later than 2010,

382
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:54,340
which is worth sitting with.

383
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:57,100
The accident board never recommended retiring the shuttle.

384
00:20:57,580 --> 00:20:59,480
It said, if you fly past 2010,

385
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:01,740
recertify it first, a conditional.

386
00:21:03,540 --> 00:21:07,280
154 days later, the White House turned that conditional into a deadline,

387
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:11,460
and the retirement decision then canceled one of the fixes the accident had called for.

388
00:21:12,580 --> 00:21:16,520
In July 2005, Discovery flew the return to flight mission.

389
00:21:17,020 --> 00:21:18,300
The tank shed foam again.

390
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:23,660
It missed. NASA grounded the fleet the following day and did not fly again for 11 months.

391
00:21:24,980 --> 00:21:27,400
Columbia itself is still at the Kennedy Space Center.

392
00:21:27,940 --> 00:21:32,440
84 ,000 pieces of it, in a room on the 16th floor of the Vehicle Assembly Building.

393
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:38,200
And they lend it out. Researchers studying how spacecraft come apart during reentry can

394
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:42,020
borrow pieces of it. Three people have written doctorates based on that debris.

395
00:21:43,520 --> 00:21:47,060
Challenger's recovered structure was sealed into a disused missile silo.

396
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:48,860
Columbia's is signed out,

397
00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:53,840
studied, and returned. It is the only destroyed spacecraft in history that is still working.

398
00:21:55,340 --> 00:21:59,780
One last thing. Elon Ramon carried a copy of a pencil drawing into orbit with him.

399
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:01,900
It is called Moon Landscape,

400
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:04,400
and it shows the Earth as seen from the Moon.

401
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:10,880
It was drawn in 1942 by a 14 -year -old boy named Peter Gins in the Taresian Stat

402
00:22:10,880 --> 00:22:15,180
Ghetto from Imagination. He was murdered at Auschwitz two years later,

403
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:20,160
aged 16. Yad Vashem made Ramon a facsimile to take with him.

404
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:22,040
The original never left Jerusalem,

405
00:22:22,460 --> 00:22:26,780
and it is still there. Peter Gins was born on the 1st of February,

406
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:30,540
1928. Columbia came apart on the 1st of February,

407
00:22:30,860 --> 00:22:33,860
2003, which would have been his 75th birthday.

408
00:22:35,220 --> 00:22:37,260
Three people asked for a photograph of that wing.

409
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:39,300
The photograph was never taken.

410
00:22:39,900 --> 00:22:41,720
Everything else in this story follows from that.

411
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:46,940
If the way an organization talks itself past a warning is the part that stays with you,

412
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:48,860
we made the other half of this story.

413
00:22:49,560 --> 00:22:51,200
Watch engineer Saeed don't launch.

414
00:22:51,580 --> 00:22:54,860
73 seconds later, Challenger was gone right here on this channel.

415
00:22:55,340 --> 00:22:58,160
And subscribe to Black Box Breakdown so you don't miss the next one.

416
00:22:59,180 --> 00:23:00,780
If this one got under your skin a little,

417
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:06,220
good, that's the point. And it only keeps happening because people like you keep showing

418
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:08,320
up. So do one thing for me.

419
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:10,340
Hit subscribe, it is free,

420
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,560
and it is the whole reason Black Box can keep taking these disasters apart.

